<!--
  This file records 8 of the ApiCompat breaks in Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub 1.3.0. They are
  the breaks caused by deliberate modelling changes in API version 2025-10-01, as distinct from the
  ones caused by the upstream compliance change to the Microsoft.ProviderHub specification. They
  fall into exactly two groups:

    - The resource-deletion-policy rename. The specification records this with @renamedFrom on
      API version 2025-10-01, so it is a service decision rather than an artifact of regeneration.
    - The promotion of OperationsPutContent to an ARM resource. The specification declares it
      Azure.ResourceManager.ProxyResource, so the flat model exposed by 1.2.x was the anomaly and
      reverting it would regress ARM conformance.

  Neither group should be mitigated back to the 1.2.x shape: doing so would contradict the
  specification. They are recorded here so the decision is reviewable.

  Measured against the 1.2.1 baseline, the regeneration produces 97 distinct ApiCompat findings.
  26 of them name an internal-only Microsoft identifier removed by the compliance change; the
  remaining 71 name nothing sensitive. ApiCompat suppression targets are fully qualified signatures
  and the tool supports no wildcard, so suppressing those 26 would reproduce in this repository the
  exact identifiers the upstream change exists to remove. They are described by location and shape
  in the package CHANGELOG.md instead. This file is therefore NOT a complete suppression list and
  could not be made into one while the compliance removals stand.

  Four findings that were previously listed here have since been fixed rather than recorded, by
  restoring the members in src/Custom: the synchronous ProviderRegistrationResource.Delete pair and
  the flattened ResourceTypeRegistrationResourceManagementOptions.BatchProvisioningSupportSupported-
  Operations property.

  NOTE: this file is currently not consumed by the build. ApiCompat is gated on a non-empty
  <ApiCompatVersion> in the project file (see eng/Directory.Build.Common.targets), which is absent
  for this package, and Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub is listed in ApiCompatVersionOptOut.txt.
  The entries below are therefore a record for reviewers, not active suppressions.
-->
